<body>
<SiteHeader active="trend" />

<main class="page page-trend">

  <section class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">The Trend &middot; the thesis, defended</p>
    <h1>The highs are higher. So are the lows.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Between 1983 and 2025, the worst US fire years got worse &mdash; but the quiet years also stopped being quiet. This page lays out the evidence, separates what the published literature already establishes from what is this project's own analysis, and features the places the data refuses to cooperate with a tidy story.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §1 THE CLAIM ============ -->
  <section id="claim">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;1</span> The claim, and who backs which part of it</h2>
    <p>The upward march of the biggest fire years is not our discovery &mdash; it is settled in the peer-reviewed and federal record, and we quote it directly. The <em>rising-floor and widening-distribution</em> half of the thesis is different: that is original analysis computed here from the official series, presented plainly as ours.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-tag">Supported by the literature &mdash; the highs</span>
      <p>The EPA's Climate Change Indicators, drawing on the same NIFC record we use, states verbatim:</p>
      <blockquote>&ldquo;of the 10 years with the largest acreage burned, nine have occurred since 2000&rdquo; <a href="#ref-epa2021wildfires">[epa2021wildfires]</a></blockquote>
      <p>and NCA5's <em>Focus on Western Wildfires</em>:</p>
      <blockquote>&ldquo;the annual area burned and area burned by high-severity wildfires have increased in the West about eightfold since 1985&rdquo; <a href="#ref-nca5_focus_wildfire">[nca5_focus_wildfire]</a></blockquote>
    </div>

    <div class="callout honesty">
      <span class="callout-tag">This project's own analysis &mdash; the floor &amp; the spread</span>
      <p>The claim that the <strong>lows rose too</strong> &mdash; that fire-year floors lifted and the whole distribution widened &mdash; is <strong>not</strong> a statement EPA or NCA5 make. They document a rising central and extreme trend, not interannual variance. The rolling percentiles, the pre/post-2000 split, and the floor-shift statistics below are computed here from NIFC annual acres (1983&ndash;2025) and presented as original analysis, with the published work used only to anchor the direction of the central trend. <a href="#ref-nca5_focus_wildfire">[nca5_focus_wildfire]</a><!-- STAT: trend_stats._meta.framing --></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §2 ERA TABLE ============ -->
  <section id="eras">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;2</span> Two eras of the same country</h2>
    <p>Split the official NIFC acreage record at 2000 and every landmark of the distribution moves up together &mdash; not just the maximum, but the minimum, both quartiles, and the median.</p>
    <div class="data-table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <caption>NIFC national acres burned, million-acre summary by era <!-- STAT: trend_stats.era_comparison_nifc_macres --></caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Era</th><th>Years</th><th>Min</th><th>P25</th><th>Median</th><th>P75</th><th>Max</th><th>IQR</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Early</td><td class="num">1983&ndash;1999 (n=17)</td>
            <td class="num">1.15</td><td class="num">1.83</td><td class="num">2.72</td><td class="num">4.07</td><td class="num">6.07</td><td class="num">2.25</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td>Modern</td><td class="num">2000&ndash;2025 (n=26)</td>
            <td class="num">2.69</td><td class="num">4.78</td><td class="num">7.29</td><td class="num">8.89</td><td class="num">10.13</td><td class="num">4.10</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="readout">
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Floor shift (minimum year)</div><div class="v">1.15 &rarr; 2.69M <small>&times;2.35</small></div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Ceiling shift (maximum year)</div><div class="v">6.07 &rarr; 10.13M <small>&times;1.67</small></div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Median shift</div><div class="v">2.72 &rarr; 7.29M <small>&times;2.68</small></div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Spread (IQR)</div><div class="v">2.25 &rarr; 4.10M <small>wider</small></div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="plain">All units million acres. The floor rose faster (&times;2.35) than the ceiling (&times;1.67): the low end of the distribution is catching up to the high end. Every one of the five lowest-acre years in the entire official record falls in 1983&ndash;1998. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.era_comparison_nifc_macres.note_lowest_years --></p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §3 ROLLING BAND ============ -->
  <section id="band">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;3</span> The band, rising</h2>
    <p>An 11-year rolling window of the NIFC series shows the same thing continuously rather than in two buckets: the p10&ndash;p90 ribbon lifts off the floor and climbs. The annual bars are the actual burned acres; the shaded band is the rolling 10th-to-90th percentile; the line is the rolling median.</p>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <div id="envelope-chart" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Rolling percentile band of NIFC annual burned acres, 1983 to 2025, showing the 10th-to-90th percentile ribbon and median rising over time, with the three 10-million-acre years 2015, 2017 and 2020 annotated.">
        <p class="data-fallback" hidden>Chart could not load &mdash; the year-by-year and era tables on this page carry the underlying values.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="figcaption">NIFC annual acres (bars) with 11-year rolling p10&ndash;p90 band + median line. Era shading at 2000. <span class="src">Source: NIFC, 1983&ndash;2025.</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="readout">
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Rolling p10 &middot; first vs last window</div><div class="v">1.32 &rarr; 4.66M</div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="k">Rolling p90 &middot; first vs last window</div><div class="v">4.62 &rarr; 10.12M</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="plain">Centered on 1988 vs 2020. The low edge of a typical decade more than tripled; the high edge more than doubled. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.rolling_band_nifc_macres.first_vs_last --></p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §4 SIGNIFICANCE ============ -->
  <section id="significance">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;4</span> Is it real, or is it noise?</h2>
    <p>Three non-parametric tests, chosen because burned-area data are skewed and we do not want to assume normality. Each is stated with a plain-language translation.</p>

    <div class="stat-line"><span class="label">Mann&ndash;Whitney U (early vs modern era)</span><span class="val">p = 1&times;10<sup>&minus;5</sup></span></div>
    <p class="plain">Plain language: if the two eras really came from the same distribution, you would see a gap this large about once in 100,000 tries. They do not. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.era_comparison_nifc_macres.mannwhitney_p --></p>

    <div class="stat-line"><span class="label">Theil&ndash;Sen slope</span><span class="val">+1.41M acres / decade &nbsp;(95% CI 0.81&ndash;2.13)</span></div>
    <p class="plain">Plain language: the typical year's burned area grows by about 1.4 million acres every ten years, and even the low end of the confidence interval is firmly positive &mdash; the trend's direction is not in doubt. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.trend_nifc.theil_sen_slope_macres_per_decade --><!-- STAT: trend_stats.trend_nifc.theil_sen_ci95_per_decade --></p>

    <div class="stat-line"><span class="label">Kendall's &tau;</span><span class="val">&tau; = 0.44 &nbsp;(p = 2.7&times;10<sup>&minus;5</sup>)</span></div>
    <p class="plain">Plain language: acres and calendar year move up together far more often than they move apart &mdash; a moderate, highly significant monotonic association. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.trend_nifc.kendall_tau --><!-- STAT: trend_stats.trend_nifc.kendall_p --></p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §5 THE 10M CLUB + CORRECTION ============ -->
  <section id="ten-million">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;5</span> The 10-million-acre club &mdash; and what it doesn't say</h2>
    <p>Three years in the entire official record crossed 10 million acres: <span class="num">2015</span> (10.13M), <span class="num">2017</span> (10.03M), and <span class="num">2020</span> (10.12M). That ceiling was never reached before 2015, then hit three times inside six years. <!-- STAT: trend_stats.ten_million_club.years --></p>

    <div class="callout honesty">
      <span class="callout-tag">The correction we feature, not bury</span>
      <p><strong>There is no new acreage record in 2021&ndash;2025.</strong> The modern peak still belongs to 2015. If the story were &ldquo;every year is worse than the last,&rdquo; this would break it &mdash; and it should. The honest thesis is narrower and better supported: a <strong>rising floor</strong> plus a <strong>ceiling first touched in 2015 and hit three times by 2020</strong>. 2023 was in fact a genuinely quiet acreage year (2.69M, ~19th percentile) &mdash; the trend is rising variance and rising floors, not monotonic escalation. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a><!-- STAT: trend_stats.ten_million_club.note --></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §6 COUNT != ACRES ============ -->
  <section id="count-acres">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;6</span> Count is not acres: the 2025 lesson</h2>
    <p>2025 makes the point cleanly. It logged <span class="num">77,850</span> wildfires &mdash; the most in a single year since 2009 (78,792) &mdash; yet burned only <span class="num">5,131,474</span> acres, landing right near the record's median (~52nd percentile of 43 years). Many fires, moderate acres. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a><!-- STAT: national_annual.nifc[year=2025] --></p>
    <p>The 2025 NIFC Annual Report says it plainly:</p>
    <blockquote>&ldquo;In 2025, the reported number of wildfires nationwide was noticeably higher than the five and 10-year averages. The number of wildfire acres nationwide was more than two million acres less than both&hellip;&rdquo; <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a></blockquote>
    <p class="plain">This is why acres, not counts, carry the trend on this page: a busy ignition year and a big burned-area year are different signals, and 2025 was the former without being the latter.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ §7 SCOPE NOTE ============ -->
  <section id="scope">
    <h2><span class="section-num">&sect;7</span> Scope, sources, and the footnotes that matter</h2>
    <h3>Two series, deliberately not merged</h3>
    <p>The national trend on this page is <strong>NIFC</strong> annual totals (1983&ndash;2025), compiled from interagency Situation Reports and including all federal and state reporting &mdash; Alaska's large tundra and boreal burns among them. The per-fire analysis elsewhere on this site uses <strong>FPA-FOD</strong> (Short 2022, 6th ed., 1992&ndash;2020), a curated per-incident dataset with its own inclusion rules. <a href="#ref-short2022fpafod">[short2022fpafod]</a> Where the two overlap they run close but do not match exactly &mdash; NIFC sits at or slightly above FPA-FOD because of its wider scope, and the small year-to-year differences flip sign rather than pointing one way. Neither is &ldquo;wrong&rdquo;; they measure different things. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a></p>

    <h3>The 1983&ndash;84 count flag</h3>
    <p>NIFC <em>acres</em> for 1983 and 1984 are fine to plot. The <em>fire counts</em> for those two years (18,229 and 20,493) are anomalously low against the 70,000&ndash;90,000 range that follows &mdash; almost certainly early Situation-Report undercounting in the first two years of the modern series, not a real collapse in ignitions. Acres, which carry this page's trend, are unaffected. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a><!-- STAT: national_annual.nifc (count_flag=true rows 1983,1984) --></p>

    <details>
      <summary>2024 fire-count footnote &mdash; a known NIFC-internal inconsistency (verbatim)</summary>
      <p>NIFC's own two products disagree on the 2024 fire count. The live &ldquo;Wildfires and Acres&rdquo; statistics table shows <strong>2024 = 64,897 fires</strong>; the 2025 Annual Report narrative says <strong>&ldquo;67,897 wildfires reported in 2024.&rdquo;</strong> The two differ by exactly 3,000 and look like a digit transposition (6<strong>4</strong>,897 vs 6<strong>7</strong>,897). We use <strong>64,897</strong> from the authoritative statistics table &mdash; the canonical annual-totals product &mdash; and disclose the report's 67,897 rather than silently picking one. Acres for 2024 (8,924,884) agree across both sources. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a></p>
    </details>

    <p class="plain">And by design, not omission: NIFC publishes no official data before 1983, because the federal wildland-fire agencies did not track wildfire data with current reporting processes before then. Every NIFC chart here starts at 1983 for that reason. <a href="#ref-nifc">[NIFC]</a></p>
  </section>

